{"maxId":273,"entries":{"chapter-36-study-gynecologic-disorders|Chapter 36 Study: Gynecologic Disorders":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Ask about bleeding and pelvic/vulvovaginal pain even when a visit addresses other concerns; review surgical, hormonal and screening history (p527–528).":["u3"],"copy|Pelvic pain often arises outside reproductive organs; assess urinary, gastrointestinal and musculoskeletal causes (p527, p530).":["u4"],"copy|Persistent or atypical vulvar lesions merit examination and possible biopsy; lichen sclerosus can scar and has malignant potential (p531–532).":["u5"],"copy|Discharge requires examination: candidiasis, bacterial vaginosis, sexually transmitted infection and atrophy have different findings (p534).":["u6"],"copy|Genitourinary syndrome of menopause may cause dryness, dyspareunia and urinary symptoms; low-dose local estrogen is a treatment option (p534–536).":["u7"],"copy|Postmenopausal bleeding requires endometrial assessment, even if other findings are present (p539).":["u8"],"copy|Sampling may be deferred at an endometrial thickness of 4 mm or less, but biopsy is preferable in older women or recurrent/persistent bleeding regardless of imaging (p539).":["u9"],"copy|Stopping cervical screening depends on adequate previous negative screening and low risk, not age alone (p536–537).":["u10"],"copy|Ultrasound/CA-125 screening is ineffective in low-risk women; investigate suspicious persistent symptoms (p527, p539).":["u11"],"copy|Most adnexal masses are benign after menopause; transvaginal ultrasound helps characterize them (p539).":["u12"],"copy|Hazzard calls initiation of systemic hormone therapy after age 65 contraindicated. It distinguishes continued treatment begun at menopause, which calls for an individualized decision (p540).":["u13"],"copy|Prolapse treatment depends on bother, overall health and goals. Observation may suit uncomplicated, minimally bothersome prolapse (p544).":["u14"],"copy|Refer for nonhealing ulceration, bleeding, retention causing recurrent infection, or hydronephrosis (p544).":["u15"],"copy|Pelvic-floor exercises and pessaries offer conservative options; assess tissue health and ability to manage the device (p544–545).":["u16"],"copy|Less mobile or cognitively impaired pessary users may need close follow-up within 1–3 months; tailor the interval to the patient (p545).":["u17"],"localizing-bleeding-pain-and-pruritus|Localizing bleeding, pain and pruritus":["u267"],"local-treatment-systemic-treatment-and-prolapse|Local treatment, systemic treatment and prolapse":["u268"],"build-the-history-around-the-organs-present-and-symptoms-the-patient-may-not-volunteer|Build the history around the organs present and symptoms the patient may not volunteer":["u269"],"symptoms-in-the-breast-and-pelvis-identify-a-lesion-that-needs-investigation|Symptoms in the breast and pelvis: identify a lesion that needs investigation":["u270"],"examine-vulvar-architecture-before-calling-persistent-pruritus-atrophy|Examine vulvar architecture before calling persistent pruritus atrophy":["u271"],"discharge-dryness-and-sex-related-symptoms|Discharge, dryness and sex-related symptoms":["u272"],"screening-is-not-the-workup-for-bleeding|Screening is not the workup for bleeding":["u273"],"characterizing-an-adnexal-finding|Characterizing an adnexal finding":["u25"],"identify-which-pelvic-compartment-is-causing-the-symptom|Identify which pelvic compartment is causing the symptom":["u26"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u27"],"copy|A negative screening history does not replace biopsy of a visibly abnormal cervix or assessment of postmenopausal bleeding (p536–539).":["u28"],"copy|A low-dose local estradiol ring and a systemic vaginal estrogen ring have different exposure profiles (p536, Table 36-3).":["u29"],"copy|A caruncle, hematuria or rectal bleeding can mimic vaginal staining; identifying one possible source does not exclude a concurrent endometrial cause (p538–539).":["u30"],"copy|Rectocele and enterocele may both produce posterior bulging; only the latter contains small intestine (p544).":["u31"],"copy|A thin endometrium on an earlier scan does not settle new or recurrent bleeding (p539).":["u32"],"copy|A pelvic mass in a symptomatic patient requires evaluation; screening an asymptomatic, average-risk woman with CA-125 is a different question (p527, p539).":["u33"],"copy|Systemic hormone therapy and low-dose vaginal estrogen have different indications, exposures and risk considerations (p534–535, p540).":["u34"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u35"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u36"],"copy|CHAPTER 36 / HAZZARD":["u19"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 36; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u20"],"copy|Assessment & vulvovaginal symptoms":["u21"],"copy|Bleeding & cancer":["u22"],"copy|Hormones & pelvic support":["u23"],"copy|Inspect the vulva, vagina and cervix while assessing postmenopausal bleeding.":["u42"],"copy|A visible local lesion may explain discomfort but should not end the evaluation for endometrial disease.":["u43"],"copy|Ultrasound and tissue sampling answer different questions: an endometrial measurement estimates risk, while biopsy can identify pathology.":["u44"],"copy|The chapter advises biopsy when bleeding persists or recurs even with a thin stripe (p538–539).":["u45"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): ACOG now recommends ultrasound plus endometrial sampling initially for most people with postmenopausal bleeding.":["u46"],"copy|Ultrasound alone is an option in selected low-risk patients with one episode, a fully seen lining no thicker than 4 mm and reliable access to immediate reassessment if bleeding recurs. - ACOG Clinical Practice Update, 2026.":["u47"],"copy|Pruritus with white plaques, bruising, fissures or loss of normal architecture suggests lichen sclerosus.":["u48"],"copy|Perianal extension may form a figure-of-eight pattern.":["u49"],"copy|Persistent or changing lesions merit inspection and consideration of biopsy.":["u50"],"copy|By contrast, diffuse dryness, burning and dyspareunia can arise from estrogen deficiency; lack of response to vaginal estrogen should trigger another look for a vulvar disorder (p531–534).":["u51"],"copy|For symptomatic atrophy the chapter describes a vaginal estradiol tablet 10 micrograms nightly for 2 weeks, then twice weekly.":["u52"],"copy|The low maintenance dose follows an initial phase of tissue remodeling.":["u53"],"copy|When the complaint is a bulge, ask about emptying the bladder, bleeding and interference with activities.":["u54"],"copy|A pessary may relieve symptoms without surgery, provided that fit, epithelial health and follow-up can be maintained (p534, p544–545).":["u55"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Starting systemic menopausal hormone therapy after age 60 or more than 10 years from menopause carries higher absolute vascular risks and requires individualized assessment.":["u56"],"copy|Selected well-informed patients may choose to start later; continuation after 65 also calls for periodic review. - North American Menopause Society position statement, 2022.":["u57"],"copy|Start with the indication for each previous operation.":["u58"],"copy|Hysterectomy does not by itself tell you whether the cervix or ovaries were removed, why the operation was performed, whether there was dysplasia, or whether earlier prolapse or incontinence resolved.":["u59"],"copy|Those details change the examination, screening decision and differential for bleeding.":["u60"],"copy|Ask separately about breast pain or discharge, vaginal staining, dryness or burning, pain with intercourse, pelvic pressure, urinary emptying, constipation and fecal leakage.":["u61"],"copy|Direct questions are important because a patient may attribute the symptom to aging or may have come to discuss a different illness (p527–528).":["u62"],"copy|Table 36-1 contrasts two approaches to the same concerns.":["u63"],"copy|Someone who can describe her symptoms may distinguish urgency from difficulty initiating urination and can explain how sexual pain affects her.":["u64"],"copy|For someone with substantial cognitive impairment, ask what she can report and supplement it with observations: new staining of the bra or underwear, guarding during perineal care, a change in pad use, infrequent hard stools or a change in behavior during toileting.":["u65"],"copy|Establish which examinations are tolerable and what question each one answers.":["u66"],"copy|A low yield from routine internal examination in an asymptomatic person is different from an examination prompted by bleeding, a new mass or pain (p527–529).":["u67"],"copy|Figure 36-1 makes one cross-system cause visible.":["u68"],"copy|A large rectal fecal impaction occupies space in the pelvis and can compress the bladder, urethra and adjacent tissues.":["u69"],"copy|A patient reporting new urinary symptoms as well as abdominal discomfort may need an examination for impaction rather than repeated empiric treatment for infection.":["u70"],"copy|Similarly, when pain persists after a gynecologic operation, verify the original diagnosis and the course of symptoms.":["u71"],"copy|The temporary relationship to surgery does not identify the organ now responsible for pain (p528–531).":["u72"],"copy|Inspect both breasts for contour, retraction, edema, erythema and changes of nipple or areola.":["u73"],"copy|Describe any palpable finding by site and relation to surrounding tissue.":["u74"],"copy|Examination cannot reliably decide whether a mass is solid or cystic, benign or malignant.":["u75"],"copy|A new lump merits assessment with imaging and pathology as indicated even if mobile or painless.":["u76"],"copy|Breast pain alone has a different differential: ask whether pressure over ribs, chest wall or cervical structures reproduces the symptom.":["u77"],"copy|The chapter includes costochondritis, radiculopathy, intercostal neuralgia and zoster among extramammary causes.":["u78"],"copy|A new postmenopausal symptom should not simply be treated with medicines used for cyclic mastalgia in younger women (p529–530).":["u79"],"copy|Clarify whether nipple discharge is spontaneous or occurs only with expression, unilateral or bilateral, and from one or several ducts.":["u80"],"copy|Spontaneous bloody or serous discharge from a single duct is pathologic.":["u81"],"copy|An intraductal papilloma is a common benign explanation, but the chapter cites an underlying malignancy risk of approximately 20% in older women with pathologic discharge even without other clinical or imaging findings.":["u82"],"copy|Eczema can mimic discharge; inspect the skin as well as the duct opening.":["u83"],"copy|Persistent breast inflammation in a nonlactating woman likewise requires reconsideration of the diagnosis rather than assuming the same cause as infectious lactational mastitis (p529–530).":["u84"],"copy|For pelvic pain, ask the patient to localize it and describe its relationship to voiding, defecation, movement and contact.":["u85"],"copy|Urinary, gastrointestinal and musculoskeletal causes are important even if the patient calls the symptom ovarian or vaginal pain.":["u86"],"copy|On the Carnett maneuver, greater point tenderness while the patient tightens her abdominal muscles supports an abdominal-wall component.":["u87"],"copy|That finding does not eliminate an internal cause.":["u88"],"copy|Palpating the pelvic floor can identify painful muscle tension, and inspection may disclose a lesion or atrophy; the workup follows the location and associated findings rather than assuming the uterus or ovaries are responsible (p530–531).":["u89"],"copy|Use Figure 36-2 to orient the description.":["u90"],"copy|Identify the labia, clitoral hood, vestibule, urethral meatus, Bartholin duct region, posterior fourchette and perineum.":["u91"],"copy|A red polyp at the urethral opening can be a caruncle; the figure compares a chronic and an inflamed lesion.":["u92"],"copy|Establish whether blood on a pad originates from this lesion, excoriated external skin, the vagina or elsewhere.":["u93"],"copy|Record pigment, erosion, scale, fissures, architectural change and the patient's site of maximal symptoms.":["u94"],"copy|A discrete lesion that persists after apparently appropriate treatment deserves another examination or biopsy (p531–532, p538–541).":["u95"],"copy|Table 36-2 organizes dermatoses by pathology.":["u96"],"copy|An eczematous or spongiotic process includes atopic, allergic-contact and irritant-contact dermatitis; ask about products touching the skin.":["u97"],"copy|An acanthotic pattern includes psoriasis and lichen simplex chronicus.":["u98"],"copy|Persistent scratching can thicken the skin and perpetuate itch, and the same reaction can overlie another dermatosis.":["u99"],"copy|Lichen sclerosus and lichen planus are lichenoid diseases.":["u100"],"copy|Vesiculobullous, granulomatous or vasculopathic processes are further alternatives when the appearance is atypical.":["u101"],"copy|The table's purpose is to prevent a nonspecific complaint such as burning from being assigned a single empiric treatment indefinitely (p531–532).":["u102"],"copy|Lichen sclerosus can produce pale porcelain-like changes, bruising, fissures and distortion around the vulva and anus.":["u103"],"copy|Look for loss of normal labial structures and narrowing that may make intercourse or examination painful.":["u104"],"copy|The book estimates malignant potential as possibly as high as 4%–5% and favors confirming the diagnosis by biopsy with at least annual visual inspection.":["u105"],"copy|A persistent focal ulcer or new induration requires reassessment even in someone with known lichen sclerosus.":["u106"],"copy|Topical estrogen may improve adjacent atrophic tissue but has little direct effect on the primary dermatosis.":["u107"],"copy|Lichen planus can produce painful erosions, involve other mucosa and also scar; specialist assessment and topical or systemic anti-inflammatory 