e.":["u139"],"copy|The separate question of initiating or continuing systemic therapy after menopause requires a review of vascular and other risks rather than extrapolation from local symptom treatment (p534–536, p540).":["u140"],"copy|Table 36-4 does not permit stopping cervical screening based on a birthday alone.":["u141"],"copy|The book combines the age or post-treatment time criterion with low risk and adequate previous negative tests.":["u142"],"copy|In its table, adequate screening means 3 consecutive negative cytology tests or 2 negative cytology/HPV cotests within 10 years, with the most recent within 5 years.":["u143"],"copy|Previous high-grade dysplasia, HIV or other immunosuppression change the decision.":["u144"],"copy|It explicitly includes an extended interval after hysterectomy performed for CIN2 or worse.":["u145"],"copy|Find the indication and pathology report before declaring screening complete.":["u146"],"copy|A visually suspicious cervix needs diagnostic evaluation even when screening would otherwise have ended (p535–537, p539).":["u147"],"copy|Table 36-5 starts with the symptom as reported rather than assuming a uterine source.":["u148"],"copy|In the uterus consider atrophy, polyps, hyperplasia, hormonal stimulation and cancer.":["u149"],"copy|A cervical polyp, cervicitis or cervical cancer can bleed.":["u150"],"copy|Vaginal atrophy, trauma, prolapse-related abrasions, a foreign body and vaginal cancer may produce similar staining.":["u151"],"copy|Vulvar ulcers, a bleeding urethral caruncle, hematuria, rectal prolapse or hemorrhoids may be mistaken for vaginal bleeding.":["u152"],"copy|Review anticoagulants and estrogen exposure, but do not let the presence of a plausible medication explanation remove the need for examination and endometrial assessment (p537–539).":["u153"],"copy|The distinction between imaging and histology is especially important after menopause.":["u154"],"copy|Identify the cervix and vaginal walls on examination; biopsy a 